From 2d73dabdea8dea370d56cac456f6d4962b7a176a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: axfor <aixiaoxiang2009@hotmail.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 22:24:11 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 22/22] lib/streamaggr: let sum_samples_total leave out flushes
 that change nothing

An aggregator flushes every output every interval, whether or not the value
moved. With scrape targets reporting increments that is mostly rewriting what
the storage already holds: measured on a rotating key set of 30k, 26180 output
series and 6723 of them -- a quarter -- carrying any increase in a two minute
window. The other three quarters are the same total written again.

output_heartbeat_interval leaves those out. A flush whose value equals the one
last written is skipped unless the heartbeat is due.

Skipping is safe for this output, and would not be for one that resets every
interval, because a change is always written. A gap therefore spans an interval
over which the value did not move, the samples on either side of it are equal,
and increase() over any window containing the gap is unaffected. Nothing is
attributed to the wrong window either: the value at the start of a gap is the
value at its end.

The heartbeat is there for a different reason. A series with no sample for
longer than the query side's lookbehind -- five minutes by default -- reads as
stale, so the interval has to stay under that, or -search.maxStalenessInterval
has to be raised to match. That is why it is a parameter rather than a fixed
choice: how far it can be pushed is a property of the query side, not of the
aggregation.

Empty or 0 keeps the old behaviour, and a value below the flush interval is
rejected rather than silently doing nothing -- an output is only written on
flush, so it could never take effect.

The flush at shutdown is not special-cased. An earlier draft did, on the
thinking that a restart needs the last value written; it does not, and no test
could be made to fail over it. If the value moved it is written like any other
change, and if it did not, the storage already holds it at the timestamp since
which it has been true.

Tests assert what is left out and that the total still arrives, rather than a
sample count: the scenario harness aligns pushes to flushes by wall clock, so
which flush a push lands in varies between runs, and a count-based assertion
fails at random. Writing a changed value only once is caught by them.

vm_streamaggr_skipped_unchanged_outputs_total counts what was left out.
